---
scope: src/service/install/scaffoldCommands
status: aligned
description: "Scaffolds the AIDE pipeline slash commands into a host project from canonical templates so the methodology's documented phases become runnable agent sessions."
intent: >
  scaffoldCommands turns the methodology's documented pipeline into the
  host project's actual runnable command surface. Each invocable phase,
  lifecycle command, and maintenance command listed in canonical doctrine
  becomes a slash command in the host, rendered byte-faithfully from
  the canonical template at install time. The category is idempotent
  per command: a command file installed previously survives untouched
  on re-runs, and per-command status is reported independently so the
  install summary names exactly which commands were new on this run.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every invocable command in canonical doctrine is covered exactly once on the host, with one-to-one coverage and no duplication. Adding a command to doctrine is the only event that grows the host's installed command surface.
    - Each installed command is a byte-faithful render of its canonical template. A diff between an installed command and its canonical source shows only the framework-specific wrapper, never paraphrases, reorderings, or summaries.
    - The category is idempotent per command. A command that already exists on the host is reported as such and left untouched; user customizations survive every re-run verbatim.
    - Per-command status is reported independently in the category's plan, so the install summary shows exactly which commands were new on this run and which were already in place.
  undesired:
    - Command content hand-written in this category's source instead of read from the canonical doc set at runtime. Same single-source-of-truth break the parent forbids.
    - "An existing host command overwritten because the canonical template is newer. The canonical template is authoritative for first install only; intentional re-sync is a separate surface, not this category's job."
    - A canonical command that fails to install on the host, or a non-canonical command installed because this category sourced membership from anywhere other than canonical doctrine.
    - A run that fails the whole category if one canonical template is missing. Each command installs independently and reports independently; one missing template is one failed command, not a cascade.
---
